\chapter{Navigating a Web}
\label{ch:navigating}

A literate web is a \emph{graph}. Output files are built from fragments;
fragments reference other fragments; and each fragment may be defined in
several pieces. The tangled code you compile is the result of walking that
graph from the \verb|@@o| roots down. The woven document (\LaTeX, Markdown,
or HTML) draws the graph for you --- around every scrap it prints the
edges, so you can see how any line of generated code was assembled and
jump to the fragments that produced it.

This chapter reads that graph \emph{by eye}. The next one
(Chapter~\ref{ch:ai}) queries the same graph \emph{mechanically}.

\section{The scrap footer: a node's edges}

Every scrap is followed by a one-line grey footer. It is the local view of
the graph --- the current node and the edges leaving it:

\begin{quote}
\begin{tabular}{@@{}ll@@{}}
\textit{N} & The scrap's own number (the node's id). \\
\(\equiv\) / \(+\!\equiv\) & A fresh \emph{definition} vs.\ a \emph{continuation} of one begun earlier. \\
\textit{defined at} \ldots & The other scraps that define this same fragment. \\
\textit{used in} \ldots & The scraps that \emph{reference} this fragment (its callers). \\
\textit{defines} / \textit{uses} \ldots & The identifiers this scrap declares or consumes (from \verb|@@|\,\ldots\,\verb|@@}|). \\
\end{tabular}
\end{quote}

\noindent So a footer reading \hbox{\it 7 $\cdot$ defined at 3, 7 $\cdot$
used in 12} says: this is scrap~7, one of two pieces (3 and~7) of a
fragment that is referenced from scrap~12. Follow \textit{used in} to go
\emph{up} the graph (towards the files that need this code); follow
\textit{defined at} to see the fragment's other pieces.

\section{Following a fragment reference}

Inside a scrap, and in the prose, a reference is drawn
\hbox{$\langle$\,\textit{Name}\ \textit{N}\,$\rangle$}: the fragment's
name followed by the number of its (first) definition. That number is the
\emph{down} edge --- it points at the scrap where the code lives. In the
HTML and hyperlinked-\LaTeX\ weaves it is a clickable link; on paper it is
the scrap number to turn to.

Reading the two directions together lets you traverse the whole web:

\begin{quote}
\begin{tabular}{@@{}ll@@{}}
\textbf{down} & a \hbox{$\langle$\,\textit{Name N}\,$\rangle$} reference $\rightarrow$ the fragment's definition. \\
\textbf{up} & a footer's \textit{used in} $\rightarrow$ the scraps that reference it. \\
\end{tabular}
\end{quote}

\noindent Start at an \verb|@@o| file, follow its \hbox{$\langle\ \rangle$}
references down into the fragments, and you have read exactly what
tangling will assemble --- in the order a human chose, not the compiler.

\section{The indices: the graph's node lists}

Three commands print the graph's nodes as navigable lists, usually near
the end of the document:

\begin{quote}
\begin{tabular}{@@{}ll@@{}}
\verb|@@f| & every output \emph{file}, with the scraps that define it. \\
\verb|@@m| & every \emph{fragment}, with its defining and referencing scraps. \\
\verb|@@u| & every indexed \emph{identifier}, with its uses (definitions underlined). \\
\end{tabular}
\end{quote}

\noindent Where the footers give the local view, the indices give the
global one: a directory of every node and a link to each. They are the
natural entry points --- pick a fragment from the \verb|@@m| index and
follow it into the narrative.

\section{A definition grown in pieces}

A fragment (or a file) may be defined by several scraps; tangling
concatenates them in source order. The \(\equiv\) marker opens the
fragment and each later piece carries \(+\!\equiv\), so a glance
distinguishes the first definition from a continuation, and the
\textit{defined at} footer lists all the pieces. This is how a header
accumulates declarations, or a switch grows case by case, across the
narrative --- the graph records every piece.

\section{From eye to tooling}

Everything above is the graph rendered for a reader. The \emph{same}
graph is available machine-readable, for editors, scripts, and AI agents:
\verb|-m| emits it whole as JSON (files, fragments, uses, transitive
dependencies), \verb|-e name --callers| prints the \emph{up}-tree for one
fragment, and \verb|--lint| checks the graph for orphans and dangling
references. When reading by eye is not enough --- a web too large to hold
at once --- reach for those; see Chapter~\ref{ch:ai}.
